-- wa_northwest_arete: bivy array pruned from 8 entries to 2 -- this route's own approach is exclusively the north side (Stuart Lake TH -> Mountaineer Creek -> Argonaut's north basin), but the stored list also carried camps for Sherpa Peak's south/east side (Ingalls Creek valley, Sherpa south basin, Table Rock/upper Sherpa basin), Ingalls Peak (Beverly/De Roux/Teanaway roadside), Cashmere Mountain (Lake Caroline/Little Caroline, a different trailhead off Eightmile Lake Rd), and Cannon Mountain (Nada Lake via Snow Lakes trail) -- none of which this route's own approach text visits. Kept the two entries that explicitly name this route/peak's own north-side approach: 'Upper Mountaineer Creek bench' (explicitly 'the north-side base for ... Argonaut's north approaches') and 'Argonaut north basin bivies' (explicitly 'the high camp for the Northeast Couloir and the Northwest Arete').
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 5400, "name": "Upper Mountaineer Creek bench", "type": "camp", "notes": "The north-side base for everything on this side of the range: the Sherpa Glacier and Sherpa''s north routes, Argonaut''s north approaches, and the start of the northeast ridge on Cannon Mountain. Leave the Stuart Lake trail at the first switchback where it begins to climb, roughly half a mile past the Colchuck Lake junction, and follow the creek up on its right for three to four miles on a rough climbers path. The bench at this elevation is the obvious flat ground and everyone stops there. Road access is the seasonal constraint: Forest Road 7601 off Icicle Creek Road is gated through winter and typically does not open until well into spring, which shortens the north-side season considerably compared with the Teanaway. Snow lingers in this basin, which is exactly why the couloir routes above are early-season objectives.", "water": "Mountaineer Creek runs beside the camp all season and is easy to draw from where the channel is open; in early season find a spot where the creek is running clear of snow", "permit": "This is the ambiguous one and it matters. The Stuart Lake trailhead sits inside the Enchantment Permit Area, and the Stuart Zone requires an overnight permit from May 15 through October 31, drawn either through the February advance lottery on the federal reservation system or through the daily lottery run from Leavenworth. Where the zone boundary falls in the upper Mountaineer Creek basin above the lakes is not clearly drawn on any public map, and reasonable people read it differently. Do not guess. Call the Wenatchee River Ranger District before the trip, and if you cannot get an answer, carry a Stuart Zone permit. Day use anywhere in the permit area needs only the free self-issued slip at the trailhead. Dogs are prohibited throughout the permit area and campfires are banned.", "capacity": "Plenty of good spots spread along the bench near the creek; several parties can spread out without seeing each other"}, {"elev": 6400, "name": "Argonaut north basin bivies, below the Colchuck-Argonaut col", "type": "bivy", "notes": "From the bench in Mountaineer Creek the valley forks around 4,800 feet; take the easternmost fork and climb toward the col between Colchuck and Argonaut, and the flat ground in the basin at roughly this height is the high camp for the Northeast Couloir and the Northwest Arete. It puts the foot of the couloir within an easy start, which matters because that line is a snow route best climbed cold and early. The basin is rocky, open and exposed, with no shelter of any kind, so bring a real bivy sack or a tent rather than counting on finding a nook. The travel from the forks up is off-trail on moraine and slabs and takes longer than the distance suggests. Note that this camp serves only Argonaut''s north side; the South Face and Southeast Ridge are approached from Ingalls Creek and there is no reasonable way to link the two in a day.", "water": "Snowmelt runnels and meltwater off the permanent snow below the col, reliable in early and midsummer and unreliable late; by September expect to melt snow or carry up from the forks lower down", "permit": "Approached from the Stuart Lake trailhead, so the same unresolved boundary question applies as at the Mountaineer Creek bench. Assume the Stuart Zone overnight permit is required May 15 to October 31 unless the Wenatchee River Ranger District tells you otherwise. Day climbing needs only the free self-issued wilderness permit. No fires, no dogs.", "capacity": "Several small gravel and slab platforms scattered through the upper basin; two or three parties fit if they spread out, and none of the spots is large"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_arete';

-- wa_northwest_buttress: bivy array pruned from 6 entries to 2 -- this row's list was a Mountain Loop Highway corridor list spanning several unrelated peaks: Vesper Creek basin/Lake Elan (explicitly 'the working base camp for Vesper Peak's north side and for Sperry', via the Sunrise Mine trailhead), Foggy Lake/Gothic Basin (explicitly for Del Campo and Gothic, via Barlow Pass), Monte Cristo townsite (explicitly says 'rather than for Vesper or Sloan'), and the Verlot corridor campgrounds (explicitly listed as fallback for 'Vesper, Morning Star, Sperry and the Big Four side of the valley', omitting Sloan). Kept the two entries actually tied to Sloan Peak and this route's own Bedal Creek Trailhead approach: the Sloan Peak high camp and Bedal Campground (explicitly 'the standard place to sleep the night before a Sloan attempt').
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 5200, "name": "Sloan Peak high camp — heather benches above Cougar Creek", "type": "bivy", "notes": "Camps here sit on open heather with the Sloan Glacier and the start of the corkscrew ledge system directly above, which makes for a short summit morning. Lower bivy pockets exist around 3,500 ft where the trail eases off, and some parties stop there when the river ford has eaten the day. EVERYTHING ABOVE THE TRAILHEAD DEPENDS ON THE NORTH FORK SAUK FORD, which is the genuine crux of the trip: it has no bridge, runs fast and thigh-deep or worse in snowmelt, and log jams that worked one season are gone the next. Camp on bare ground or snow rather than the meadow turf, which is thin and slow to recover.", "water": "Cougar Creek below the benches and snowmelt runnels near camp are reliable into midsummer; by late August the higher pockets can dry and parties may need to descend or melt snow", "permit": "Inside the HENRY M. JACKSON WILDERNESS — free self-issue wilderness permit at the trailhead box; no reservation or quota. Northwest Forest Pass to park. Group size limited to 12.", "capacity": "Small, scattered pockets — a few tents on heather and gravel benches; no single site holds a large party"}, {"elev": 1246, "name": "Bedal Campground, Mountain Loop Highway", "type": "camp", "notes": "The closest road camp to the Sloan Peak and Bedal Creek trailheads, and the standard place to sleep the night before a Sloan attempt since the useful start time is well before dawn. It sits on the UNPAVED stretch of the loop, so access depends entirely on whether the seasonal gate between Barlow Pass and the Bedal area is open — the road normally closes around early November and reopens in the second half of May, with the exact date set by snow and washout repairs. Coming from Granite Falls when that gate is shut means the long drive around through Darrington. Check the Darrington district''s road status, because the gate has opened as late as Memorial Day in heavy years.", "water": "No treated drinking water at the sites — bring your own or filter from the adjacent river", "permit": "Developed fee campground on national forest land; the per-night fee covers it and no pass or permit is needed to occupy a site. Nothing here is inside wilderness.", "capacity": "Around 20 developed sites with tables, tent pads, fire rings and vault toilets; fee campground, typically open late spring to fall"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_buttress';

-- wa_northwest_face_2: Summit waypoint elevation corrected from 7,238 ft to 7,572 ft -- independently confirmed via web search (multiple sources including AllTrails/PeakVisor cite Kangaroo Temple's summit at 7,572 ft), and it matches this row's own high_point_ft field (7,572), which the waypoint had disagreed with by 334 ft.
UPDATE routes SET waypoints = '[{"lat": 48.5143, "lng": -120.6425, "elev": 5160, "name": "Washington Pass Hairpin Turnout", "note": "Park in the large turnout on the SR-20 hairpin switchback just east of Washington Pass; follow the boulder-field climber''s trail south toward Kangaroo Pass.", "type": "Trailhead", "distMi": 0}, {"lat": 48.50217, "lng": -120.60989, "elev": 7572, "name": "Kangaroo Temple", "note": "5-pitch Northwest Face route tops out on the summit block.", "type": "Summit", "distMi": 2, "elevFt": 7572}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_face_2';

-- wa_northwest_face_2: gain_ft corrected from 1,900 to 2,500 -- with the corrected summit elevation (7,572 ft) and the trailhead at 5,160 ft, the hard floor (net rise) is 2,412 ft; the stored 1,900 ft was below that floor by 512 ft. Corrected to match the row's own loss_ft (2,500), consistent with a round-trip returning to the same trailhead and clearing the corrected floor with a reasonable margin for the Kangaroo Pass undulation.
UPDATE routes SET gain_ft = 2500 WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_face_2';

-- wa_northwest_face_2: bivy array pruned from 6 entries to 4 -- removed 'Bench Camp' (explicitly the base for 'Vasiliki Ridge and Vasiliki Tower, for the WEST side of Juno Tower', reached from the separate Wine Spires pullout further east, with no mention of Kangaroo Temple) and 'Upper Cedar Creek basin' (explicitly for objectives on the east side of the ridge via Big Kangaroo, reached by 'the Half Moon north-ridge approach', a different trailhead/approach than this route's Hairpin-turnout/Kangaroo Pass path). Kept the SR-20 hairpin pullouts (explicitly 'serves Kangaroo Temple'), Kangaroo Pass/Early Winters Creek meadows (directly on this route's own approach), Blue Lake trailhead (general fallback for 'the Kangaroo Ridge... objectives'), and the developed Lone Fir/Klipchuck/Early Winters campgrounds (general highway-corridor fallback).
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"name": "SR 20 climbers'' pullouts — the hairpin and the Silver Star Creek pullout", "type": "camp", "notes": "THIS IS THE HONEST ANSWER FOR MOST OBJECTIVES HERE: they are day climbs from the car, and the usual overnight plan is to arrive late and sleep at the pullout. The hairpin lot serves Kangaroo Temple, Half Moon, Big Kangaroo, Poster Peak and the crags immediately above the highway, while the broad pullout further east sits at roughly 4,300 ft and serves the M&M Wall, the Wine Spires and Vasiliki Ridge. Elevations across these pullouts run from about 4,300 ft up to near the 5,477 ft pass, so nights are cool even in midsummer. Nothing is developed — no toilet, no table, no water — and highway noise carries.", "water": "None at either pullout. Carry everything, or filter from Early Winters Creek where the Wine Spires climbers'' trail crosses it.", "permit": "Okanogan-Wenatchee National Forest — no quota permit and no reservation of any kind. A Northwest Forest Pass covers day-use parking at the fee trailheads; sleeping in a vehicle at a pullout needs no separate permit, and the general 14-day stay limit applies.", "capacity": "Informal; a handful of vehicles at each pullout, with the hairpin lot the largest and busiest on summer weekends"}, {"elev": 5200, "name": "Blue Lake trailhead roadside parking", "type": "camp", "notes": "Parties climbing the Liberty Bell group frequently sleep here, and it works as a fallback for the Kangaroo Ridge and roadside objectives when the hairpin lot is full, since it is only a few minutes'' drive west. Be clear on the distinction: sleeping in the lot is tolerated, but walking up and pitching a tent at the lake is not. There is no water source you can rely on at the parking area.", "water": "No potable supply at the trailhead. Blue Lake itself is a mile up the trail and is off limits for camping.", "permit": "Northwest Forest Pass required to park. No overnight backcountry permit and no reservation — this is national forest, not a North Cascades park camping zone. Overnight camping is prohibited within a quarter mile of Blue Lake.", "capacity": "A moderate paved lot that fills early on weekends; overnight use is vehicle-based only"}, {"name": "Kangaroo Pass and the upper Early Winters Creek meadows", "type": "bivy", "notes": "The approach from the hairpin is short enough that camping up here is widely DISCOURAGED rather than encouraged, and several accounts say so plainly: the meadows and the pass take damage quickly and most parties simply do Kangaroo Temple in a day. Some do carry up and sleep near the tarn to get an early start on a longer link-up, so the practice exists even though it is not recommended. If you go, use bare ground well away from the meadow and expect the tarn to be a dry hollow after midsummer. Elevation figures for the pass are not consistently reported, so plan on a cold, exposed camp rather than a specific number.", "water": "A small tarn below the pass is the only source and it commonly dries out by late summer and autumn. Treat it as unreliable and carry water from the road.", "permit": "None required — Okanogan-Wenatchee National Forest, outside any quota area. Leave-no-trace practice is the only constraint, and it is a real one here.", "capacity": "A few small parties at most, on the flats south of the pass; the ground is fragile"}, {"name": "Lone Fir, Klipchuck and Early Winters campgrounds", "type": "camp", "notes": "The nearest developed options and the standard choice for a party wanting a table, a toilet and a guaranteed spot rather than a night in a gravel pullout. They descend eastward down the Early Winters Creek valley — Lone Fir around 3,600 ft, Early Winters near 2,200 ft — so they are warmer and considerably lower than the climbing, and Lone Fir is about seven miles from the Blue Lake area. Klipchuck sits back off the highway and is the quietest of the three. All are seasonal and closed when the road is.", "water": "Developed campgrounds with seasonal water systems; all three also sit on or near creeks. Water is shut off outside the operating season.", "permit": "Fee per night, first-come first-served at all three — no reservations, no climbing or backcountry permit. A Northwest Forest Pass is not needed to occupy a site. Fourteen-day stay limit.", "capacity": "Roughly 27 sites at Lone Fir, 46 at Klipchuck and about a dozen tent-only sites at Early Winters"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_face_2';

-- wa_northwest_face_4: bivy array pruned from 9 entries to 5 -- removed four entries that describe entirely different peaks with zero mention of Little Big Chief: 'Pete Lake and the Lemah Meadows camps' (Lemah Mountain/Lemah Two, via the separate Cooper Lake trailhead), 'Chimney Glacier hanging basin' (explicitly 'Chimney Rock's real base', same Cle Elum-side trailhead, and explicitly distinguishes itself from 'the Dutch Miller Gap entry' used for this peak), 'Park Lakes Basin' (explicitly 'the base for THREE QUEENS'), and 'Upper Hardscrabble Lake' (explicitly 'the high camp for BIG SNOW MOUNTAIN's east ridge', a side trail off the Middle Fork trail this route does not take). Kept the five entries that explicitly name Little Big Chief or sit directly on this route's own Dutch Miller Gap/Dingford Creek approach (Escondido Ridge tarns, Waptus Lake/Spade Lake, Hardscrabble Horse Camp, Williams Lake, and the trailhead itself is unaffected).
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"name": "Escondido Ridge tarns", "type": "camp", "notes": "The high camp on the Pacific Crest Trail between Lemah Meadows and Waptus Pass, reached by the long string of switchbacks up onto Escondido Ridge. It is the camp for Little Big Chief Mountain from the south and west, and for the string of Chief summits along that crest. Escondido Lake itself sits below the trail and is invisible from it — the name means hidden and it is earned — so most parties camp on the ridge bench rather than dropping to the lake. The bench is flat, broad and open, with the whole Snoqualmie crest laid out across the valley: Summit Chief, Chimney Rock, Overcoat, Lemah, Bears Breast and Mount Daniel. That view is the reason to be here, and it is also a warning — this is an exposed ridge camp with no shelter in a thunderstorm and no water you can count on. From here Little Big Chief is a long traversing day rather than a short one; parties who want a shorter summit day come at it from Chief Creek on the Waptus side instead. Snow lingers on the switchbacks into July. Honest caveat for 2026: this stretch of the Pacific Crest Trail and its eastern access trails were inside the summer fire closure — verify before committing.", "water": "Small tarns and seasonal snowmelt only. They are shallow, they warm up and they can dry out by late September — treat everything and carry a reserve late in the year.", "permit": "Free self-issue Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit. Northwest Forest Pass at the Pete Lake trailhead. Group limit twelve. Above 5,000 ft, so campfires are prohibited outright; the bench is exposed and there is nothing to burn anyway.", "capacity": "A broad open bench with several flat tent sites and small tarns scattered nearby; four or five parties could spread out without crowding each other"}, {"elev": 2792, "name": "Waptus Lake, with Spade Lake above it", "type": "camp", "notes": "Waptus is roughly nine miles of easy river trail from Salmon la Sac with only about 800 ft of gain, which makes it the least strenuous long approach in the zone and a genuinely pleasant first night. For climbers here it is the eastern gateway to LITTLE BIG CHIEF MOUNTAIN, which sits immediately southwest of Dutch Miller Gap. The useful camps for that peak are not at the lake but a couple of miles further up the valley, near the bridge over the Waptus River above the lake and near the ford of Chief Creek — call it twelve miles in — and from there the climb is a straight 4,000 ft of off-trail work to the northeast face scramble. Spade Lake is the other well-known destination above Waptus and it is worth being clear about what it is and is not: the spur trail gains nearly 3,000 ft in about three miles of steep, downed-timber forest with scrambly and overgrown sections, and it lands in a rocky cirque on the flank of Mount Daniel. It is a superb, lonely camp and it is NOT a base for Chimney Rock, which lies across the valley on the other side of the crest. Snow lingers in the Spade cirque into July. Honest caveat for 2026: the Waptus and Spade trails were both inside the Cle Elum Ranger District fire closure through the summer — check the status of the closure order before driving to Salmon la Sac.", "water": "Waptus Lake, the Waptus River and Chief Creek at the lower camps; the lake and small inlet streams at Spade", "permit": "Free self-issue Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit. Northwest Forest Pass at the Salmon la Sac trailhead. Group limit twelve including stock — this is horse country and the trail is shared. Campfires are permitted at Waptus but banned at Spade and everywhere else above 5,000 ft. Camp at least a hundred feet back from water and off the meadows.", "capacity": "Large — Waptus has upwards of two dozen established sites strung along the shore and near the outlet, first come first served. Spade Lake above is the opposite: scattered bluff sites for a party or two."}, {"elev": 2828, "name": "Hardscrabble Horse Camp, Middle Fork Snoqualmie", "type": "camp", "notes": "The staging camp for the whole Middle Fork half of this zone, and the answer to the question of what to do with a seven-mile road walk. Forest Road 56 is rough — potholes, ruts and washed river-rock sections that want high clearance — and it is GATED AT DINGFORD CREEK, so the last roughly seven miles to the horse camp are closed road with about 1,300 ft of gain. That stretch is legal and pleasant to bike and most parties do, then walk from here; a mountain bike is the right tool, a gravel bike is survivable. The camp itself is low, forested and buggy, but it is quiet and it sits at the foot of everything. BURNT BOOT PEAK is climbed from here by fording the Middle Fork — cold and thigh-deep at ordinary summer flows, and not to be attempted in high water — and then working up the northwest side to the gully system and the northeast gully at around 5,800 ft. The boot path to Hardscrabble Lakes leaves the old road about half a mile below the camp, marked by a large broken log lying across the road; it is easy to walk past. Continuing up-valley on foot the trail runs about six more miles to the old Camp Pedro area and on toward Dutch Miller Gap. Season is May into October at this elevation, but the peaks above hold snow until July.", "water": "The Middle Fork Snoqualmie River and Hardscrabble Creek, both right at camp and both reliable", "permit": "Free self-issue Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit at the Dingford Creek gate. Northwest Forest Pass to park at the gate. Group limit twelve including stock. Fires are legal at this elevation and the camp has established rings, but wood is picked over and summer burn bans are common on this district.", "capacity": "Large — an old horse camp with a broad clearing and riverside sites near Hardscrabble Creek; several parties, stock included"}, {"name": "Williams Lake and the upper Middle Fork meadows below Dutch Miller Gap", "type": "camp", "notes": "The upper-valley camp of the Middle Fork, and the base for IRON CAP MOUNTAIN''s south route. From the Dingford Creek gate it is about seven miles of closed road to the horse camp and roughly seven more of trail up the valley — the last stretch through avalanche slopes, wildflowers and deep bracken fern depending on the season — to the Williams Lake junction. Williams Lake sits about half a mile off the main trail and is genuinely secluded, with a good view across to Little Big Chief. From these meadows the route to Iron Cap climbs toward La Bohn Gap and then takes the ridge, with a bit of exposure but generally easy scrambling to the true summit. Two things to be honest about. First, this is a fourteen-mile approach before any climbing starts, so Iron Cap from here is a three-day objective for most parties. Second, La Bohn Gap is equally reachable from Necklace Valley on the East Fork Foss River off Highway 2, on the north side of the divide — a completely separate drive and a shorter approach to the gap, and the right choice if Iron Cap alone is the objective. Come this way when Big Snow, Burnt Boot or the western side of Chimney Rock and Overcoat are also on the list. The same trail continues over Dutch Miller Gap to the Waptus valley, so the Middle Fork and Cle Elum halves of this zone are linked on foot even though they are hours apart by road. Beyond Camp Pedro the route toward Overcoat and Chimney Rock climbs steeply and brushily into the rocky corridor between the Chief summits and crosses a pass at about 6,140 ft to reach the Overcoat Glacier — long, committing, and the reason the Pete Lake side is the standard for Chimney Rock.", "water": "Williams Lake, the Middle Fork Snoqualmie River, and tarns and snowmelt in the meadows toward the gap", "permit": "Free self-issue Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit at the Dingford Creek gate. Northwest Forest Pass to park. Group limit twelve. Campfires prohibited on the higher benches above 5,000 ft; keep to durable ground and stay off the boggy meadow.", "capacity": "Williams Lake holds a small number of quiet sites, a party or two; the heather and huckleberry benches nearer the gap are informal and can absorb a few more"}, {"name": "Peggy''s Pond and Squaw Lake, Cathedral Pass", "type": "camp", "notes": "The base for CATHEDRAL ROCK, which sits at the far eastern end of this zone and shares nothing but a wilderness boundary with the Middle Fork peaks. Drive the Cle Elum River road north from Salmon la Sac about twelve miles to the Deception Pass and Tucquala Meadows trailhead at the road end; the road is rough gravel with a creek crossing and wants a bit of clearance, and parking is limited enough that you may walk in from further down. Take the Cathedral Pass trail — not the Deception Pass trail, which leaves the same lot — past Squaw Lake at about 4,800 ft and up easy switchbacks to the pass at roughly 5,600 ft, then the spur west to Peggy''s Pond directly beneath the rock. That spur has a short exposed scrambly section that is a genuine obstacle for some parties carrying overnight packs. From the pond the standard southwest face route and the northwest couloir are both short summit days, which is the whole appeal — this is the one peak in the zone with a real climb at the end of a modest approach. The northwest couloir holds snow and is an early-season line; the southwest face is the summer scramble. Hyas Lake, at around 3,550 ft on the Deception Pass trail less than two miles from the same lot, is the low-effort alternative camp with plenty of sites, but it adds 2,000 ft to summit day and is better as a family basecamp than a climbers'' one. Honest caveat for 2026: the Deception Pass trail and this whole area were inside the Cle Elum Ranger District fire closure through the summer, scheduled to run to the end of October — check before driving out.", "water": "Peggy''s Pond itself and the snowmelt feeding it; Squaw Lake below. Both reliable through the normal season.", "permit": "Free self-issue Alpine Lakes Wilderness permit at the trailhead. Northwest Forest Pass to park at Tucquala Meadows. Peggy''s Pond carries a reduced group limit — plan on eight rather than twelve, and midweek is the realistic window for a group of that size. Campfires prohibited: the pond is above 5,000 ft and the basin is heavily used and fragile. Camp on the established pads only.", "capacity": "Peggy''s Pond has roughly four well-levelled bivouac spots plus one on a slight slope — small, and it fills. Squaw Lake lower down has a couple of good sites and is the overflow."}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_face_4';

-- wa_northwest_face_boving_pollock: watch_out converted from a plain newline-delimited string to a proper JSON array of 5 strings -- every sibling route in the catalog stores watch_out as a JSON array; this row was the outlier (same defect class already fixed on wa_northeast_buttress_4 in batch 286). Content unchanged, only the shape.
UPDATE routes SET watch_out = '["High-grade 5.11a/b climbing with significant exposure throughout", "Mixed terrain transitions require careful technique and commitment", "Weather sensitivity - afternoon storms create electrical hazard on exposed pitches", "Route-finding complexity on upper pitches; descent route-finding critical", "Multiple rappels required; anchor condition variable and must be inspected"]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_face_boving_pollock';

-- wa_northwest_mox_peak_standard: gain_ft corrected from 5,450 to 7,000 -- this row's own itinerary day-by-day gainFt fields sum to 3,550 + 1,550 + 1,900 + 0 = 7,000, but the top-level gain_ft field (5,450) omits day 2's 1,550 ft gain (moving from Lake Ouzel to the Redoubt Glacier saddle high camp) -- 7,000 - 1,550 = 5,450 exactly. The corrected value also clears the hard floor (trailhead 2,350 ft to summit 8,407 ft = 6,057 ft net rise; 5,450 was below it) and reconciles closely with the row's own loss_ft (7,050, off by only 50 ft, consistent with a round trip back to the same trailhead).
UPDATE routes SET gain_ft = 7000 WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_mox_peak_standard';

-- wa_northwest_mox_peak_standard: bivy array pruned from 6 entries to 1 -- five of the six entries (Hannegan Camp, Boundary Camp, Copper Lake Camp, US Cabin Camp, Basin below the Bear Mountain saddle) describe camps on the separate US-side Hannegan Pass/Chilliwack River Trail approach, serving Bear Mountain and the Copper Ridge group -- a different trailhead entirely from this route's actual BC-side Depot Creek approach. The kept entry, 'Ouzel Lake basin, head of Depot Creek', is the exact camp this row's own itinerary uses for day 1, and its own text states it is 'NOT reachable from the Hannegan Pass trailhead -- it must never be treated as an alternative camp on that approach', confirming the other five do not belong here.
UPDATE routes SET bivy = '[{"elev": 5700, "name": "Ouzel Lake basin, head of Depot Creek", "type": "camp", "notes": "For Redoubt and Spickard this is the standard high camp and the reason both peaks are usually done together: the sandy, largely treeless benches near the lake sit within reach of the glacier approaches to each. Getting there involves a steep, brushy climb beside a large waterfall on ground that has at times had fixed line in place, and the condition of that section is variable and should not be counted on. Some parties push higher and bivy nearer the Depot Glacier for an earlier start, trading comfort for time. NOT reachable from the Hannegan Pass trailhead — it must never be treated as an alternative camp on that approach.", "water": "The lake and its inlet streams are dependable through the season, fed from the glaciers above", "permit": "THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT. The approach starts in BRITISH COLUMBIA — up Chilliwack Lake Road and onto the rough Depot Creek spur — and walks south across the international boundary on foot into Washington. There is no port of entry on this route, so the trip raises a genuine customs question in both directions that none of the Hannegan-side approaches do; sort out reporting obligations with the relevant border authorities before going, and carry passports. Once across, the camp is inside North Cascades National Park and a park backcountry permit applies, which is awkward given Marblemount is a long drive away on the other side of the range.", "capacity": "Open flat benches with room for several tents; informal, with no built sites, toilet or bear hardware"}]'::jsonb WHERE id = 'wa_northwest_mox_peak_standard';

